@page "/form-datetime"

<CatHead Title="Date and time">
    <code>.form-input</code> on a date, time, month or week input. The box is the text field's;
    what the class adds is the parts the browser draws in its own colours, and the two things
    iOS does to a date input that a desktop never shows.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.Basics" Title="The four inputs" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>date</code>, <code>time</code>, <code>datetime-local</code> and <code>month</code>,
        each a <code>.form-input</code>. Pick the type for the picker and the keyboard the platform
        gives you, and keep <code>value</code> in the wire format &mdash; <code>2026-09-18</code>,
        <code>08:30</code> &mdash; whatever the reader's locale shows. A
        <code>datetime-local</code> is local to the browser and carries no zone: the hint says
        which zone the server will read it in, or two people book the same hour.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.Range" Title="From and to" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Two dates in a <a href="/grid"><code>.sedna-grid</code></a> inside one
        <code>.form-section</code>, so the legend names the pair. <code>min</code> and
        <code>max</code> are what the picker greys out; move the end's <code>min</code> with the
        start, or the picker offers a range that the server will reject.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.Steps" Title="Slots and weeks" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>step</code> in seconds turns a time input into slots &mdash; 900 is a quarter hour
        &mdash; and <code>min</code>/<code>max</code> bound the day. <code>week</code> is for a
        planning horizon; its value is the ISO week, <code>2026-W38</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The input's own tiers, <code>.form-input--sm</code> and <code>-lg</code>, so a date beside
        a button is the button's height. A date is a fixed shape, so it takes a
        <a href="/form-text"><code>.sedna-w</code></a> width rather than filling the row.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.States" Title="Read-only, disabled and rejected" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same three states as a text field: <code>readonly</code> is still submitted,
        <code>disabled</code> is not, and a rejected date carries <code>aria-invalid</code> and a
        <a href="/form-validation"><code>.form-error</code></a> that says what a valid one would be.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.Typeable" Title="A typeable date with a calendar" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A text field people type into &mdash; <code>3.11.26</code>, <code>03112026</code> &mdash;
        beside a button that opens a <a href="/calendar"><code>.datepicker</code></a>, as one
        <code>.input-group</code>. Input that cannot be read as a date sets
        <code>aria-invalid</code> on the input, and the group draws the rejected edge. Opened from
        the button, the picker would line up with the button at the group's end; an
        <code>anchor-name</code> on the group and the same name as the popover's
        <code>position-anchor</code> line it up with the field instead, and it still flips at the
        screen's edge.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.TimeRange" Title="A time range with quick select and auto-refresh" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open both.</strong> A dashboard or a log asks for a span of time, and
        most of the time it is a relative one. There is no range-picker class: an
        <code>.input-group</code> holds a button that opens the quick select and a
        <a href="/form-text"><code>.form-trigger</code></a> showing the range, which opens a
        <a href="/calendar"><code>.datepicker</code></a> for an absolute start and end, with the
        range marked by <code>--range-start</code> and <code>--range-end</code> and the times in a
        <code>.datepicker-time</code> row. The quick select is a <code>.popover</code>: a relative
        range built from a select, a number and a unit, the common ranges as
        <a href="/chip"><code>.chip</code></a> buttons with <code>aria-pressed</code> on the one in
        force, the reader's recent ranges as <code>.btn-bare--link</code> buttons, and the
        auto-refresh interval with a button that starts and stops it. The group is the anchor of
        both panels, named by <code>anchor-name</code> and each panel's
        <code>position-anchor</code>. The app keeps the range, the recent list and the timer; the
        refresh button says what interval is running.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="memorable-dates">Dates the reader already knows <CatAnchor Id="memorable-dates" /></h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    A date of birth or the day a business started is typed faster than it is found in a picker,
    and a picker that opens on today makes the reader page back through decades. Ask for it as
    separate day, month and year fields in a <code>.form-section</code>, each
    <code>type="text"</code> with <code>inputmode="numeric"</code>, sized to its answer with
    <code>.sedna-w-fit</code> and <code>size</code>, under a legend that names the date.
</p>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.AskDateOfBirth" Title="Date of birth" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>bday-day</code>, <code>bday-month</code> and <code>bday-year</code> let a phone fill it
        in. The hint gives an example in the order the fields come; accept a month as
        <code>3</code>, <code>03</code> or <code>March</code>, and a day without its leading zero.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormDatetime.AskDateErrors" Title="When a date is incomplete" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One message for the whole date, under the hint, naming what is missing or wrong: &ldquo;must
        include a year&rdquo;, &ldquo;must be in the past&rdquo;, &ldquo;must be a real
        date&rdquo;. <code>aria-invalid</code> goes only on the fields that are wrong, so the reader
        is taken to the year and not to the month they already gave.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="platform">What the browser draws</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    The calendar glyph is a browser glyph in a fixed colour, invisible on a dark surface until it
    is inverted &mdash; <code>--picker-invert</code> flips it with the theme &mdash; and the picker
    panel follows <code>color-scheme</code>, set on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> from
    <code>--color-scheme</code>. On iOS a date input has an intrinsic width and centres its value,
    and both ignore the class, so the field ran past its column and its text sat in the middle;
    the stylesheet gives it <code>min-width: 0</code> and <code>appearance: none</code> and starts
    the value where every other field's starts. The picker still opens on tap.
</p>
